Because a String cannot be changed in place, convert it to a char array and swap characters from the two ends moving towards the middle. When the two pointers meet, the array holds the reversed text, and you build a new String from it.

```java
public String reverse(String input) {
    char[] chars = input.toCharArray();
    int left = 0, right = chars.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        char temp = chars[left];
        chars[left] = chars[right];
        chars[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return new String(chars);
}
```

This runs in O(n) time and O(n) extra space for the char array. In real code you would just use new StringBuilder(input).reverse().toString(), but interviewers ask for the manual version to check that you can work with indexes and swapping. Mention the empty-string and single-character cases: both return unchanged, with no special handling needed.

Split the sentence into words, then count each word in a HashMap. getOrDefault keeps the counting to one line: look up the current count (0 if the word is new), add one, and store it back.

```java
public Map<String, Integer> countWords(String sentence) {
    Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
    for (String word : sentence.toLowerCase().split("\\s+")) {
        counts.put(word, counts.getOrDefault(word, 0) + 1);
    }
    return counts;
}
```

For the sentence "java is fun and java is powerful", this returns {java=2, is=2, fun=1, and=1, powerful=1}. Lowercasing first stops 'Java' and 'java' being counted separately. Note the limits: punctuation stays attached to words ('fun,' is not 'fun'), so a stricter version would strip punctuation first, and the map does not preserve the order words first appeared in — use a LinkedHashMap if order matters.

A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards, so compare characters from both ends moving towards the middle. The first mismatch means it is not a palindrome; if the pointers meet with no mismatch, it is one.

```java
public boolean isPalindrome(String input) {
    int left = 0, right = input.length() - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (input.charAt(left) != input.charAt(right)) {
            return false;
        }
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return true;
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(1) extra space. For a case-insensitive check, compare Character.toLowerCase on each side instead of the raw characters. The same two-pointer idea works for a number: convert it to a string first, or reverse its digits arithmetically and compare with the original — but watch out for overflow and for negative numbers, which are never palindromes because of the minus sign.

Walk the array once, keeping track of the largest value seen and the second-largest distinct value. When a number beats the largest, the old largest becomes the second-largest; when it sits between the two, it becomes the new second-largest. Duplicates of the largest are ignored, so [9, 9, 7] gives 7, not 9.

```java
public int secondHighest(int[] nums) {
    Integer first = null, second = null;
    for (int n : nums) {
        if (first == null || n > first) {
            second = first;
            first = n;
        } else if (n != first && (second == null || n > second)) {
            second = n;
        }
    }
    if (second == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Need at least two distinct numbers");
    }
    return second;
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(1) space. The null-based tracking avoids sentinel-value bugs. If the input is guaranteed to have two distinct values you can drop the exception, but naming the edge case (fewer than two distinct numbers) is exactly what interviewers want to hear.

Collectors.groupingBy sorts stream elements into buckets by a key you choose, and returns a Map from key to list. Add a downstream collector to transform each bucket — counting() turns each list into a count, and mapping() or summingInt() can summarise it another way.

```java
// How many employees are in each department?
Map<String, Long> byDepartment = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department, Collectors.counting()));

// Group words by their length
Map<Integer, List<String>> byLength = words.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
```

The plain one-argument form groups into lists; the two-argument form applies the downstream collector to each group. groupingBy is an eager terminal operation — it consumes the whole stream and builds the map — and the default map is a HashMap, so groups come back in no particular order. If you need sorted keys, pass a TreeMap supplier with the three-argument overload.

The trick is to keep insertion order while counting: group the characters with Collectors.groupingBy, supplying a LinkedHashMap so entries stay in first-seen order, and count each group with Collectors.counting(). Then stream the map entries, keep the first one whose count is one, and return its key, or null when every character repeats. The counting pass touches each character once and the scan touches at most the distinct characters, so the whole thing is linear in the length of the string, with memory proportional to the number of distinct characters. A plain HashMap would give the right counts but the wrong 'first', because iteration order is not insertion order — that is the most common mistake. A simpler two-pass alternative counts with a map and then scans the original string for the first character whose count is one; it is equally linear and easier to read, and it handles the empty-string and all-repeated cases by returning null.
Example solution:

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FirstNonRepeated {
    public static Character firstNonRepeated(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        Map<Character, Long> counts = input.chars()
                .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Function.identity(), LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));
        return counts.entrySet().stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getValue() == 1)
                .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
                .findFirst()
                .orElse(null);
    }
}
```

Use a dummy node at the front so you never need special code for the first element: keep a tail pointer starting at the dummy, and while both lists have nodes, link the smaller current node after the tail and advance that list and the tail. When one list runs out, link the rest of the other list as it is — it is already sorted, so no further comparison is needed — and return dummy.next as the merged head. The dummy node is the key idea: without it, the first link needs a separate branch and the code gets messy. The merge reuses the existing nodes, so it uses constant extra space beyond the dummy node, and it touches each node once, giving linear time in the combined length. Using <= when comparing keeps the merge stable, so equal values keep their original relative order. A recursive version is shorter but risks a stack overflow on long lists, so prefer the iterative version in production code; also handle null inputs, since a null list just means the other list is the answer.
Example solution:

```java
public class MergeSortedLists {
    static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    public static Node merge(Node first, Node second) {
        Node dummy = new Node(0);
        Node tail = dummy;
        while (first != null && second != null) {
            if (first.value <= second.value) {
                tail.next = first;
                first = first.next;
            } else {
                tail.next = second;
                second = second.next;
            }
            tail = tail.next;
        }
        tail.next = (first != null) ? first : second;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

The design is a small buffer with a fixed capacity, a put method that waits while the buffer is full, and a take method that waits while it is empty, both synchronized on the same monitor. Waiting must happen inside a while loop that re-checks the condition after waking: a thread can wake spuriously, or another consumer can take the last item before this thread runs again, and an if would let it proceed on a false assumption and fail. After adding an item, put calls notifyAll so a waiting consumer wakes; after removing one, take calls notifyAll so a waiting producer wakes. notifyAll is preferred over notify here because producers and consumers wait on different conditions on the same monitor, and waking one arbitrary thread may wake the wrong kind. A fresh implementation looks like this:

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

class BoundedBuffer<T> {
    private final Queue<T> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    private final int capacity;

    BoundedBuffer(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    public synchronized void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.size() == capacity) {
            wait();
        }
        queue.add(item);
        notifyAll();
    }

    public synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.isEmpty()) {
            wait();
        }
        T item = queue.remove();
        notifyAll();
        return item;
    }
}
```

In production code you would usually use a BlockingQueue instead, which provides this behaviour already tested, but interviewers ask for wait and notify to check that you understand monitors, lost wakeups, and why the condition loop matters.

A behaviour test has three steps: arrange the mocks, act by calling the service method, and assert by verifying the interactions. When the service builds the entity itself, for example by stamping the current time or copying fields from a request, you cannot write the expected object in advance, so you capture the real argument instead. ArgumentCaptor takes the object the mock actually received so you can assert on its fields, while verify with times(1) checks the call happened exactly once. A fresh example looks like this:

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.times;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.verify;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.when;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.mockito.ArgumentCaptor;
import org.mockito.InjectMocks;
import org.mockito.Mock;
import org.mockito.junit.jupiter.MockitoExtension;

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private OrderRepository repository;

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderService service;

    @Test
    void placesOrderWithDiscountApplied() {
        when(repository.save(org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.any()))
                .thenAnswer(invocation -> invocation.getArgument(0));

        service.placeOrder("BOOK-1", 2);

        ArgumentCaptor<Order> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Order.class);
        verify(repository, times(1)).save(captor.capture());
        assertEquals("BOOK-1", captor.getValue().itemCode());
        assertEquals(2, captor.getValue().quantity());
    }
}
```

Reach for a captor when the argument is constructed inside the code under test; when you already know the exact object, a plain verify with that object is simpler and reads better. Keep the test focused on one behaviour: a test that verifies five interactions at once becomes brittle and fails whenever the implementation changes without the behaviour changing.

This is a grouping problem, not a sorting problem: count first, filter second. Collect the list into a map from value to count with groupingBy and counting(), then stream the entries, keep the ones with a count above one, and collect them back into a map. A compact version:

```java
public static Map<Integer, Long> duplicatesWithCounts(List<Integer> numbers) {
    return numbers.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()))
        .entrySet().stream()
        .filter(entry -> entry.getValue() > 1)
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));
}
```

For List.of(4, 2, 7, 4, 9, 2, 4) this returns {4=3, 2=2}. If the input can be large, point out that this is a single pass with one map, so it is linear time and linear in the number of distinct values for memory. A common follow-on is to do the same for strings, for example finding duplicate words, which needs no change except the element type. Sorting the result by count descending is a one-line extra step with a comparator on the entry value, and interviewers often ask for it next.

This is a nested collector: groupingBy splits the stream by department, and maxBy picks the winner inside each group. Because a group could in theory be empty, maxBy returns an Optional, so the result is a map from department to Optional employee. A clear version:

```java
public record Employee(String name, String department, double salary) {}

public static Map<String, Optional<Employee>> topEarnerByDepartment(List<Employee> employees) {
    return employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department,
            Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparingDouble(Employee::salary))));
}
```

Two follow-on moves show seniority. First, unwrap the Optional with collectingAndThen and Optional::orElseThrow when you can argue a group is never empty, or keep it and let the caller decide. Second, name the alternatives: the same result can be built with toMap and a merge function that keeps the higher salary, which is shorter but harder to read, while groupingBy plus maxBy states the intent directly. Also mention ties: maxBy keeps the first maximum it meets, so if two people share the top salary the winner depends on encounter order, and a real requirement would need a tie-break rule.

